{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"chewed-tinned-akitainu","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Problem\n\nThe continuous auto-deploy restarts the review-bot container while a code review is in\nflight, and the interrupted review casts a fail-closed `LLM-Review -1`. That vote then\nBLOCKS the automatic recovery which already exists, so every occurrence costs a human\nre-trigger.\n\nObserved FOUR times in a single session.\n\n### Evidence — the collision\n\n`infra/systemd/rebar-autodeploy.timer` fires every 2 minutes (`OnUnitActiveSec=2min`), and\n`rebar-autodeploy.service` makes the box reflect `main` by rebuilding the review-bot\ncontainer. Correlating each review window (patch-set upload -> coverage-gap vote, all UTC)\nagainst commits landing on `main`, THREE OF FOUR failures had a commit land mid-review:\n\n| change | review window | `main` commit inside it | offset from start |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1132 | 20:02:44 -> 20:31:36 | 20:11:50 `cd78` | +9m06s |\n| 1189 | 23:24:51 -> 23:39:03 | 23:26:15 `6758` | +1m24s |\n| 1193 | 00:17:01 -> 00:22:11 | none | — |\n| 1208 | 16:21:15 -> 16:41:48 | 16:24:04 `5200` | +2m49s |\n\n(1193 is unexplained and should be accounted for during implementation — a deploy can be\ntriggered by something other than a merge.)\n\n### Evidence — why the drain does not save it\n\nA drain EXISTS: `stop_grace_period: 150s` on the review-bot service. But it is sized for\nthe review's TAIL, not its body. Per its own derivation comment the 150s is\n45s `SHUTDOWN_DRAIN_SECONDS` + 60s store write-lock + 45s push reconcile lock, and its\nstated purpose is that \"an in-flight STORE WRITE is never killed midway\" (motivated by the\n2026-07-31 rebar-autodeploy-errors lock-orphaning incident).\n\nMeasured review durations from Gerrit (STARTED -> LLM-Review vote):\n\n    ch1199  260s     ch1194  819s     ch1189  841s     ch1154  911s\n\nSo reviews run 260-911s against a 45s drain window. A redeploy landing mid-review kills the\nreview with 5-20x the work remaining.\n\n### Evidence — the vote suppresses the existing safety net\n\n`src/rebar/review_bot/reconcile.py` is a backfill reconciler built for exactly this case.\nIts docstring: \"if a webhook delivery is lost (RECEIVER RESTART, transient network) the\npatchset is never voted and — because submit REQUIRES the vote — the change sits\nunsubmittable forever. This poller closes that loop.\" It finds patchsets whose CURRENT\nrevision has NO `LLM-Review` vote and re-invokes `voter.review_and_vote`. It runs on\nSTARTUP (`app.py:90` launches `reconcile_loop` in the lifespan) and every\n`RECONCILE_INTERVAL_SECONDS` (default 300, `config.py:228`). Double-voting is prevented by\na shared single-flight lock + dedup ledger and Gerrit's authoritative `has_llm_review_vote`\n(`reconcile.py:210`).\n\n**Because the reconciler keys on \"no LLM-Review vote\", casting the fail-closed -1 makes the\nchange invisible to it.** The veto does not merely add noise — it disables the recovery\ndesigned for this failure. That is why change 1208 sits at -1 instead of self-healing, and\nwhy one earlier instance flipped to +1 on re-review against BYTE-IDENTICAL code.\n\n## Change 1 — auto-deploy hourly, not every 2 minutes\n\nMove `infra/systemd/rebar-autodeploy.timer` from a 2-minute cadence to hourly. This cuts\nthe collision window by roughly 30x.\n\nTradeoff to state explicitly in the change: `main` can then be up to an hour stale on the\nbox, which slows how quickly a review-bot fix reaches production. Decide and document\nwhether an on-demand deploy path (a manual `systemctl start rebar-autodeploy.service`) is\nthe intended escape hatch for an urgent fix.\n\n## Change 2 — fail-NO-VOTE for retryable conditions\n\nAn interrupted or infrastructure-failed review must leave the change VOTE-LESS so the\nbackfill reconciler re-drives it, instead of casting a fail-closed -1 that suppresses\nrecovery.\n\n`docs/review-policy.md:47-52` already enumerates the tag vocabulary. The split to\nimplement:\n\n* **Retryable -> NO VOTE** (leave vote-less, let backfill re-review):\n  `review-error`, `llm-unavailable`, `scanner`, `gate-disabled`, and process teardown\n  mid-review.\n* **Terminal -> VOTE -1** unchanged: a real `finding`.\n* `indeterminate` needs a judgement call during implementation — decide whether a genuine\n  \"could not determine\" conclusion is retryable or terminal, and record the reasoning.\n\nThe critical discriminator is between \"the review reached a genuine indeterminate\nconclusion\" (vote, correctly fail-closed) and \"the process was torn down mid-review\" (do not\nvote). That boundary likely sits between the drain cancelling the worker task and the\nvoter's exception handling — see `voter.py` around the fail-closed conversion (~:174,\n`_merge_coverage_gap_decision` ~:245) and `app.py`'s shutdown path\n(`SHUTDOWN_DRAIN_SECONDS`, post-drain cancel bounded by `SHUTDOWN_CANCEL_SECONDS`).\n\n### Two constraints the fix MUST respect\n\n1. **No silent-pass hole.** Vote-less must remain unsubmittable. If `events-log` is\n   unavailable the reconciler degrades (`RECONCILE_DEGRADED`) and relies on the webhook; in\n   that state an unvoted change stays unvoted = unsubmittable = still fail-closed. Verify no\n   degraded mode turns \"no vote\" into \"submittable\".\n2. **Genuinely vote-less.** The reconciler pre-filters on the CURRENT revision having no\n   vote, so nothing provisional may be posted on the change or the backfill is re-suppressed.\n\n### Bounded retry — do not create an invisible infinite loop\n\nIf an infra failure is PERSISTENT (misconfigured credentials, a durable LLM outage), a pure\nno-vote leaves the change silently unreviewed and retrying forever with nobody alerted. The\nimplementation must bound this: after N attempts on the same revision, escalate — either\ncast the -1 (so a human sees it) or fire an alarm. Note that\n`infra/terraform/monitoring_88ab.tf:35` already defines a `review_bot_merge_change_errors`\nCloudWatch alarm that may be the right escalation surface.\n\n## Relationship to bb9b\n\n`bb9b-608f-0183-4ca5` gives contributors a way to re-trigger a fail-closed -1 manually.\nThis ticket reduces how often that is needed, by not casting the vote in the first place and\nletting the existing backfill recover automatically. They are complementary: bb9b remains\nthe manual escape hatch for the residual and for the bounded-retry escalation above.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- AC1: `rebar-autodeploy.timer` runs hourly; the staleness tradeoff and the on-demand deploy\n  path are documented in the unit file or the runbook.\n- AC2: A review interrupted by process teardown casts NO vote and leaves the change\n  vote-less.\n- AC3: The retryable coverage-gap reasons listed above cast NO vote; a real `finding` still\n  casts -1. `docs/review-policy.md`'s tag table is updated to state which reasons vote.\n- AC4: A test proves a vote-less change left by an interrupted review IS picked up by\n  `reconcile` on the next pass and reviewed to a real verdict.\n- AC5: A test proves no degraded mode makes a vote-less change submittable — fail-closed is\n  preserved.\n- AC6: Retries on one revision are bounded, and exhausting the bound escalates visibly\n  (vote or alarm) rather than retrying silently forever.\n- AC7: make lint and make typecheck pass by exit code; the default selection is green.","id":"0347-b2cc-e6e9-4f6b","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Stop auto-deploy killing live code reviews: hourly cadence, and fail-NO-VOTE instead of fail-closed for retryable conditions"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785691226408178001,"uuid":"d805402d-7d36-4819-ae1d-d0c7882acab1"}